//? Title: Rotate loadings to correlated factors
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare pattern and structure after oblique rotation

Six synthetic measurements have orthogonal-model loadings on two factors.
Oblimin reduces overlap between squared loadings, with quartimin as its
default criterion. Promax first applies varimax and then fits a
signed-power target that emphasizes larger loadings.

Both methods return a pattern matrix \(P\) of loading coefficients, the
factor correlation matrix \(\Phi\), and the structure matrix
\(S=P\Phi\). For standardized measurements, the structure entries are
the correlations between each measurement and each factor. An indicator
can have a small pattern coefficient on the second factor and still
correlate with it through the first factor.

```{cpp, id=oblique-pattern-and-structure, file="figures/oblique_rotation.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/factor_analysis.hpp>

#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    namespace dm = datamunge;
    const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> input(6, 2, {
        .78, .21,
        .68, .30,
        .73, .26,
        .32, .74,
        .25, .81,
        .36, .62,
    });
    const auto quartimin = dm::stats::oblimin(input);
    const auto promax = dm::stats::promax(input);
    if (!quartimin.converged || !promax.converged)
        throw std::runtime_error("Expected both rotations to converge");

    const auto common = input * input.transpose();
    std::println("| Rotation | Factor correlation | A1 pattern on Factor 2 | A1 structure on Factor 2 | Maximum change in common covariance |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto report = [&](const char* name, const dm::stats::ObliqueRotation& fit) {
        const auto difference = fit.structure * fit.pattern.transpose() - common;
        double max_deviation = 0;
        for (const double entry : difference.data())
            max_deviation = std::max(max_deviation, std::abs(entry));
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.1e} |", name,
            fit.factor_correlation(0, 1), fit.pattern(0, 1), fit.structure(0, 1),
            max_deviation);
    };
    report("Oblimin (quartimin)", quartimin);
    report("Promax (power 4)", promax);

    dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(57, 750);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle latent;
    latent.circle = true;
    latent.width = latent.height = 1.5;
    latent.fill = {224, 239, 252};
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle indicator;
    indicator.width = 1.35;
    indicator.height = .7;
    diagram.node("F1", {2, 3.2}, "Factor 1", latent)
        .node("F2", {8.4, 3.2}, "Factor 2", latent)
        .link("F1", "F2", std::format("{:.2f}", quartimin.factor_correlation(0, 1)));
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < input.rows(); ++i) {
        const std::size_t factor = i / 3;
        const auto name = std::format("{}{}", factor == 0 ? 'A' : 'B', i % 3 + 1);
        diagram.node(name, {2.0 * (i % 3) + 6.4 * factor, 0}, name, indicator)
            .edge(factor == 0 ? "F1" : "F2", name, std::format("{:.2f}", quartimin.pattern(i, factor)));
    }
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    diagram.save_svg("build/oblique_rotation.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Rotation | Factor correlation | A1 pattern on Factor 2 | A1 structure on Factor 2 | Maximum change in common covariance |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Oblimin (quartimin) | 0.624 | -0.053 | 0.471 | 1.1e-16 |
| Promax (power 4) | 0.658 | -0.081 | 0.485 | 1.7e-16 |
\image(figures/oblique_rotation.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Quartimin solution. The connection between factors shows their correlation. Arrows show the six primary pattern coefficients.)
\alttext(Factor 1 points to A1, A2, and A3. Factor 2 points to B1, B2, and B3. A horizontal line between the factors shows their positive correlation.)

In this example, both methods give A1 a small secondary pattern
coefficient and a larger secondary structure coefficient. The common
covariance remains \(P\Phi P^\top\) for either rotation, equal within
roundoff to the common covariance before rotation.

[Jennrich (2002)|https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/psychometrika/article/abs/simple-general-method-for-oblique-rotation/4159E602B694E2FB13D66E565AF73011] · [Hendrickson and White (1964)|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8317.1964.tb00244.x]
